            <ArticleTitle>Long-term disability associated with flail chest injury.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>324-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Twenty-two trauma victims who had sustained flail chest as their only significant injury were evaluated to determine the final outcome. Fourteen patients (63.9 percent) were found to have long-term sequelae. The most common long-term problems after flail chest injury were persistent chest wall pain, chest wall deformity, and dyspnea on exertion. Five patients (22 percent) remained disabled in varying degrees.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
